WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:12.520
STEPHANIE WONG: Hi,
everyone, and welcome.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.300
I hope you're enjoying
Google Cloud Next '21.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.040
I know this is
another virtual year.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:21.060
But I'm hoping that today, I get
to bring some of my experiences

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.890
to life for you.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.330
I'm going to be
talking about what

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.330
it was like to explore the six
layers of Google Cloud data

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.890
center security.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:32.159
Now, you might be thinking of
lasers, night vision cameras,

00:00:32.159 --> 00:00:34.590
and military-grade fences.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.450
You're not too far
off because security

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.270
is one of the most critical
elements of our data center's

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:41.940
DNA and Google Cloud workloads.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.010
With dozens of data
centers globally,

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.500
security operations
means managing

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.870
a massively complex network.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:51.850
I was one of the rare
Googlers to visit one.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:53.640
So I'm going to take
you on a journey

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.620
to the core of a data
center to show you

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.930
the six layers of
physical security designed

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:01.200
to thwart unauthorized access.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.890
But first, I'll touch on Google
Cloud security philosophy

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.410
before I take you on that
journey to the core of the data

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:07.932
center.

00:01:07.932 --> 00:01:09.390
And then we'll
touch on how you can

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:13.987
leverage that same
philosophy on Google Cloud.

00:01:13.987 --> 00:01:15.320
And it's great to be here today.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:16.520
My name is Stephanie Wong.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.100
I'm a Cloud developer advocate.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.530
And I've been creating
developer-focused content

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.990
for the last four
years or so here.

00:01:22.990 --> 00:01:25.540
Of course, when I got the
chance to visit a data center

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.670
and make a film about it, I
jumped at the opportunity.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:33.170
So let's go ahead and
dive right into it.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.380
So in terms of
transparency, we're

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:38.680
very clear about our commitments
to customers about your data.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.040
Rule number one-- your data
belongs to you, no one else.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:44.620
We do not sell customer
data to third parties,

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.450
nor do we use it
for advertising.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.610
Your data is your data.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.030
Your data is also encrypted in
transit and at rest all times.

00:01:52.030 --> 00:01:53.810
And you do not have
to ask for that.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:55.360
And if you want, we
can give you ways

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.040
to add additional
encryption on top of that.

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.960
We also have explicit rules to
guard against insider access.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:04.390
There is no back door to Google.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.310
There's no secret
government access.

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:09.070
We take pride in our
international audits.

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:12.023
And we deliver
results on request.

00:02:12.023 --> 00:02:13.440
These are the most
important roles

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.480
in our mission of
digital transformation.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.160
It's giving you the trust that
you need to build great things.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:21.830
So how can Google Cloud
help organizations

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.450
with their security?

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:25.910
Well, first, we provide
protection from threats

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:28.640
through a secure foundation
core infrastructure

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.400
that is designed, built, and
operated with security in mind.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.390
Second, we provide customers
with a constantly expanding

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:37.310
array of controls
that they can use

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:41.060
to help meet policy, regulatory,
and business objectives.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.300
And third, we work to meet our
compliance responsibilities

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.850
and provide capabilities
to make compliance easier

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.650
for customers.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.200
But first, let's talk
about the protection

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:56.780
that you get from building on
our infrastructure foundation.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:59.480
Our infrastructure doesn't
rely on any single technology

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:00.740
to make it secure.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:04.220
Rather, we build security
through progressive layers that

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.560
deliver true defense in depth.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.410
So our hardware is
Google-controlled, built,

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:10.520
and hardened.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:13.640
Any application binary that
runs on our infrastructure

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:15.500
is deployed securely.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.200
We do not assume any
trust between services.

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:21.200
And we use multiple mechanisms
to establish and maintain

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:21.950
trust.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:23.540
Our infrastructure
was designed to be

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.850
multi-tenant from the start.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.310
All identities,
users, and services

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.050
are strongly authenticated.

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:31.790
Data stored on
our infrastructure

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:33.770
is automatically
encrypted at rest

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:37.670
and distributed for
availability and reliability.

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.550
Any communications over the
internet to our Cloud Services

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:41.960
are also encrypted.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:44.090
And the scale of
our infrastructure

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.720
allows us to absorb many
denial of service attacks.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.790
We have multiple
layers of protection

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:54.210
that further reduce the
risk of any DDoS impact.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:57.470
And finally, our operations
teams detect threats

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:02.040
and respond to incidents
24/7, 365 days a year.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.730
We actually have a white paper
written on our infrastructure

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:07.940
design that goes into all
of these areas in much

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:09.230
more detail.

00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:11.510
Other cloud providers might
describe a similar stack

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:12.650
of capabilities.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.290
But the way that Google Cloud
approaches many of these,

00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:18.540
in my opinion, is very unique.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.649
So I had the chance to
visit a data center in 2019.

00:04:21.649 --> 00:04:23.420
And I made a video
about my experience

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:24.738
that you should all check out.

00:04:24.738 --> 00:04:26.030
I'll be sure to share the link.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:29.840
But you can find it
at bit.ly/6layersdeep.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:31.280
This was a huge project.

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:34.190
And it wasn't easy to
get approvals or even

00:04:34.190 --> 00:04:36.770
step foot inside the premises.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:39.410
But in my job at
Google, I spend my days

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:40.910
working with developers.

00:04:40.910 --> 00:04:44.000
Our data centers are crucial
to the work that they do.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.330
But most never actually set
foot inside a data center.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:51.260
So as you can see here, there
is a huge amount of space

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:56.480
that our data centers span,
352,000 square feet, or 33,000

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:57.560
square meters.

00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.110
And all in all, less
than 1% of Googlers

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:02.030
ever get to set foot inside.

00:05:02.030 --> 00:05:05.150
So I was determined to make a
video because for close to two

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:07.400
decades, Google
has designed data

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:09.170
centers from the ground up.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:12.260
Security has surfaced as one
of the most critical elements

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:13.280
of our DNA.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.100
And it's part of our commitment
to keep our customers'

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:19.310
data safe, and not to
mention Google Cloud

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:22.910
now meets more than 45
regulatory requirements,

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.700
and growing.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.610
Our products regularly undergo
independent verification

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:30.880
of their security, privacy,
and compliance controls,

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:34.120
achieving certifications,
attestations of compliance,

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:37.550
or audit reports against
these global standards.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:39.580
This means that an
independent auditor

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:42.580
has examined the controls
present in our data centers,

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:44.770
infrastructure, and operations.

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:47.530
These certifications include
some of the most widely

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:51.070
recognized, internationally
accepted independent security

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:56.890
standards, including ISO/IEC
27001 for security controls,

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:00.700
ISO/IEC 27017 for
Cloud Security,

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:04.450
and ISO/IEC 27018
for cloud privacy

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:07.990
as well as AICP SOC 1, 2, and 3.

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:09.730
These certifications
are helping us

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:12.430
meet the demands of
the industry standards,

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:16.630
like CSA STAR and
PCI DSS as well

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:20.240
as many other regional
standards as well.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:22.840
We continue to expand our
list of compliance offerings

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:26.380
globally to assist our
customers with their compliance

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:28.410
obligations.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:32.270
So while auditors can sometimes
enter our data centers

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:35.840
to perform these audits, most
people, including Googlers,

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:36.830
cannot.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.500
Under special
authorization, I found out

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:41.810
that our physical
data center security

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:45.260
is built using that defense
in depth foundation.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:48.110
It's made up of six
layers, from the exterior

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:52.160
all the way to the center,
each increasing in controls

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:53.900
as you get to layer 6.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:06:56.450
So I'm going to walk
through each layer and some

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:59.120
of the most notable
features and experiences

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.670
that I went through.

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:03.250
So let's start with layer 1.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:05.110
This is the secure perimeter.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:07.690
Basically, it means the
property boundaries.

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:09.880
And that includes all
the signage and fencing

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.030
around the property.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:14.480
These are the furthest, most
exterior edges of the property.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:16.360
So as you drive
into the property,

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.990
you'll see signs that might
have a Google logo or a signage.

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:22.540
But otherwise, you really
can't tell immediately

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:23.920
that's-- it's a data center.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:25.150
It's, more or less--

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:29.670
looks like an industrial
area or a business park.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.420
But things really start
to get interesting

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:34.960
when it comes to layer 2, also
known as the secure perimeter.

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.140
And that includes the
main entrance gate.

00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:41.000
So I started my
journey by driving up

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.980
to this gate, at
which point I needed

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.470
my credentials and
my identity checked.

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:48.050
Even before stepping foot
into the property boundaries,

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:51.140
I was already on an
access approval list.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.990
The least privileged
protocol is the idea

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:56.870
that someone should have only
the bare minimum privileges

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:59.150
necessary to perform their job.

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:02.090
So if your least privilege
is to enter layer 2,

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:05.390
then you're not going to
have luck moving to layer 3.

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:07.070
Each person's access
and permissions

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:09.560
are checked at badge readers,
starting with layer 1,

00:08:09.560 --> 00:08:12.650
and progresses more
stringently at layer 6.

00:08:12.650 --> 00:08:15.290
These exist at every access
point in the data center

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:16.160
facility.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.320
And authorization
measures happen everywhere

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:19.730
using this protocol.

00:08:19.730 --> 00:08:22.610
This is something that we don't
just employ in a data center,

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:24.590
but it's also a best
practice we always

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:28.160
recommend for how you set up
your organization resources

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.050
and data on Google Cloud.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:33.020
So as you know, applying
least privilege to IAM roles

00:08:33.020 --> 00:08:34.700
on Google Cloud is recommended.

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:37.909
So any user has the bare minimum
roles and their permissions

00:08:37.909 --> 00:08:39.900
to perform their functions.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:42.350
So layer 2 has a lot
of security features,

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:45.410
ranging from smart fencing
to overlapping cameras

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.020
to 24/7 guard patrols and more.

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:50.240
And there's actually
a lot more technology

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.940
and operations going on behind
the scenes than meets the eye.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:56.000
So from the time
that you're on-site,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.810
they already know
that you're there.

00:08:57.810 --> 00:08:59.630
But what I found
also interesting

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:02.510
is that they're able to do
correlation analysis of where

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:05.600
you've been on site through
cameras, badge access

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.170
tracking, et cetera.

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:08.930
And there are also
guards in vehicles

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:10.490
and some guards on foot.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:14.180
As you can see here, there's
also the vehicle crash barrier.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:17.270
This is designed to stop a
fully loaded truck from crashing

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:19.430
through the front entrance.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:22.190
This particular black fence
also that you see here

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:24.110
is an anti-climb fence.

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:26.300
So there are spikes
pointed outward.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:29.550
It'd be pretty hard to get
over that in one piece.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.640
It's also equipped with fiber.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:34.820
The technology tells data
center security operations

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:38.180
if someone's near the
fence or touches the fence.

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:41.060
They also use thermal
cameras and standard cameras

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:44.120
so that they're able to see
video footage at night just

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.760
as clearly as they
can during the day.

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:49.430
Each camera overlaps the
area that it can cover.

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:51.410
So there's no blind
spots anywhere

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.120
around the data center.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.370
Now, you might be
wondering, well,

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:57.680
what if somebody skydives
into the property

00:09:57.680 --> 00:09:59.390
or digs under the ground?

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:01.797
Trust me, they have enough
coverage of the area

00:10:01.797 --> 00:10:03.380
that they will detect
something coming

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:05.810
into the property
or motion that's

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:07.940
occurring near the fence.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.190
These are not only
physical safeguards,

00:10:10.190 --> 00:10:12.320
but act as a big
deterrent to anyone

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.560
even thinking about trying
to forcibly come inside.

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:19.160
And more obstacles
await them inside.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:21.200
And we have additional
operational monitoring

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:24.140
happening in any case that
will alert more guards

00:10:24.140 --> 00:10:27.110
to take action if
they detect something.

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:29.540
Layer 3 is building access.

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:32.240
In this case, I was
able to enter the lobby.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.370
But just so you
know, this is still

00:10:34.370 --> 00:10:36.260
nowhere near the
data center floor

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:38.970
because that's still a
few more layers deep.

00:10:38.970 --> 00:10:41.390
So let's say that you've
gotten through the gate,

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:44.960
you've come inside, you've
come into the secured lobby,

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:47.750
and you have your card or a
form of identification, which

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:50.030
is checked by a receptionist
to make sure that you're

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:52.810
on the access control list.

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:55.690
But what happens if
you lose your card?

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:57.690
Then what?

00:10:57.690 --> 00:11:01.320
For that reason, biometric
scanning is also employed.

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:04.620
So, yes, I did have to
get my irises scanned.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:06.930
Similar to concepts in
software development,

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:10.260
biometric scanning is for
authentication of identity.

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:13.200
Meanwhile, my badge allowing
access to a certain area

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:15.030
is authorization.

00:11:15.030 --> 00:11:17.400
Yes, this also felt
like I was on some sort

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:21.090
of secret top mission, maybe
"Mission Impossible" or maybe

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:22.920
"Ocean's Eleven"
or, more recently,

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:25.530
I've been hearing it's
been compared to Professor

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:27.450
from "Money Heist."

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:30.900
But once that was taken
care of, another rule

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:32.760
exists at the data
center that prevents

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:34.920
a vehicle or an
individual closely

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:38.430
following another to gain
entry into a restricted area

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.380
without a badge swipe.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:43.590
So if the system detects
a door open for too long,

00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:46.240
it immediately alerts
security personnel.

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:49.020
Any gate or door must close
before the next vehicle

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:52.560
or the person can badge
in and then gain access.

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:55.050
You've probably seen
dual authentication

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:56.760
when you try to
sign into an account

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:59.790
and you get a one-time
password sent to your phone.

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:01.380
We take a similar
approach at the data

00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:04.710
centers to verify a person's
identity and access.

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:06.330
At some layers in
the data center,

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:08.460
you're required to
swipe your badge

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:14.160
and then enter a circle
lock or a tubular doorway.

00:12:14.160 --> 00:12:16.350
You walk into this
special half-portal

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:19.500
that checks your badge and
scans your eyes to gain access

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:21.480
to the next layer
of the data center.

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:24.090
It prevents anyone from
closely following you

00:12:24.090 --> 00:12:27.480
through because only one person
is allowed into the circle lock

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:29.110
at a time.

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:32.580
So you can see here that I
have to first badge, then

00:12:32.580 --> 00:12:34.860
step inside, get
my irises scanned,

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:37.740
and then it verifies my identity
and the door on the other side

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:39.940
unlocks.

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:42.280
And something not shown
in the video that I made

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:44.120
is the facility loading
docks, which are

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:46.030
a special section of layer 3.

00:12:46.030 --> 00:12:48.610
They're used to receive and
send shipments of materials,

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:50.050
like new hardware.

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:52.720
Truck deliveries from vendors
must be approved for access

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:55.090
to layer 3 to enter the dock.

00:12:55.090 --> 00:12:57.460
For security, the
loading dock room

00:12:57.460 --> 00:13:00.280
is physically isolated from
the rest of the data center.

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:02.380
And a guard is present--

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:04.000
a guard presence
is required when

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:06.135
a shipment is received or sent.

00:13:06.135 --> 00:13:08.260
I actually got super lucky
when I was there and got

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:11.980
to witness shipments being made
of new servers being delivered.

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:14.050
And in order for them
to even get to layer 3,

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:17.050
they had to have been on
the allowed access list

00:13:17.050 --> 00:13:22.100
through layers 1 and 2
before arriving at layer 3.

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:24.500
Guards checked their
identification and the access

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:27.440
list before letting them into
layer 2 and, subsequently,

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:30.980
opening the garage door to let
them back up into the loading

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:32.150
dock room.

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:35.570
Then out came fresh new
server racks, one by one.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:38.240
And once unloading was
complete, the vendor left

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:39.920
and the hardware
operations team began

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:43.340
to move the racks into layer
4 by badging themselves in,

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:46.700
one at a time, with the actual
hardware going into the data

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:48.080
center.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:50.420
Badge checks occur
at every entry point

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:51.890
throughout the
facility to ensure

00:13:51.890 --> 00:13:53.960
least privilege is adhered to.

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:56.150
Even timed
authorizations happen.

00:13:56.150 --> 00:13:59.700
So you can do your job for only
an allotted amount of time.

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:03.020
So this could apply to loading
docks or even performing

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:05.520
hardware maintenance on
the data center floor.

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:07.520
You're probably thinking
of your favorite movie,

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:11.660
wondering, what if supply
chain gets intercepted?

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:14.030
Well, additional checks happen
for this potential risk.

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:16.130
And we have tested for
many potential risk

00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:19.010
scenarios, which I'll talk
about in a little bit.

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:22.310
Layer 4 is once you've actually
entered the data center itself.

00:14:22.310 --> 00:14:26.120
And this includes an extremely
important part of data center

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:29.870
security, the Security
Operations Center, or SOC,

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:32.120
a hive of activity that's
monitoring the data

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:36.810
center 24/7, 365 days a year.

00:14:36.810 --> 00:14:40.140
The doors, the cameras, the
badge readers, iris scan--

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.090
everything is
monitored from here.

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:44.460
Anyone on site at
the facility will

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:47.250
have their movement patterns
tracked throughout the property

00:14:47.250 --> 00:14:49.560
via their badge
and biometric scans

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:51.330
at entry points and doors.

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:54.180
If a door is kept open for
too long or correlation

00:14:54.180 --> 00:14:56.070
analysis finds that
their presence is

00:14:56.070 --> 00:14:59.850
in an unauthorized location,
alerts will be sent to the SOC

00:14:59.850 --> 00:15:01.020
instantly.

00:15:01.020 --> 00:15:04.840
This is the brains of
the security system.

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:07.980
So if there's anything out
of the ordinary happening,

00:15:07.980 --> 00:15:10.880
they have to be able
to pick that up.

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:13.040
The SOCs of all
Google data centers

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:16.700
are also designed to
roll up to a regional SOC

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:19.850
for multi-regional
high-availability purposes.

00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:22.370
If an on-site SOC
were to be disrupted

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:25.430
due to an event, such as a
natural disaster or emergency

00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:28.190
building evacuation,
monitoring instantly

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:31.520
rolls over to the regional SOC
for continuous, uninterrupted

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:32.960
monitoring.

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:36.080
SOCs act as a center
for emergency and safety

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:39.420
response in addition to
security responsibilities.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:42.020
These SOCs monitor
and respond to alarms

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:44.600
at all of our facilities
and are constantly

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:48.800
tracking local and global events
that could impact operations

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:50.300
at our data centers.

00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:52.190
Think of this like
high availability

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:53.660
for physical security.

00:15:53.660 --> 00:15:56.840
And really, that also means
it's high availability

00:15:56.840 --> 00:16:00.940
for logical security and
data protection as well.

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:03.340
Layer 5 is the
data center floor.

00:16:03.340 --> 00:16:06.850
Servers and network equipment
that power Google live here.

00:16:06.850 --> 00:16:10.150
Security in this layer is one
of the most highly restrictive,

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:12.590
as it's where the data resides.

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:15.400
And this meant I needed
to enter a circle lock,

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:17.950
get my irises scanned
and badge checked.

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:20.470
And then once in there, I
was accompanied by our guards

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:21.610
in every space.

00:16:21.610 --> 00:16:24.250
And the VP of global
data centers, Joe Kava,

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:26.890
took me around.

00:16:26.890 --> 00:16:29.620
So only hardware operations
personnel, security

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:32.080
professionals, and
key people granted

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:35.380
access on an as-needed
basis can enter.

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:37.810
Access is limited to the
time required to complete

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:39.590
the necessary job function.

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:43.190
So this is truly an
as-needed-only access area,

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:45.460
meaning that only the
technicians and engineers that

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:48.490
have to be there to
maintain, upgrade, or repair

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:51.550
the equipment are
ever allowed there.

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:54.400
This layer is protected by a
circle lock, as I mentioned,

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:57.220
security staff, and
badge-protected doors.

00:16:57.220 --> 00:16:59.980
And the data center floor
features laser beam intrusion

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:01.030
detection.

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:03.550
Additional cameras are also
placed throughout the area

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:07.900
while security staff monitor the
data center floor at all times.

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:11.920
And the technicians who
have access to the devices

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:12.530
are there.

00:17:12.530 --> 00:17:14.829
But the data at
rest is encrypted.

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:18.819
And you can also issue and
keep your own encryption keys.

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:20.530
And if you're
wondering, Googlers

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:24.430
do not have access to the data
unless you've authorized it

00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:25.930
for a technical issue.

00:17:25.930 --> 00:17:28.160
And this is called
access transparency,

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:29.680
which is a technical control.

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:33.280
It produces immutable logs any
time a Google administrator

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:36.380
needs to enter a customer's
tenant to perform something,

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:38.800
whether it's a support
request or maintenance.

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:42.490
And you control what types of
access that you want to permit.

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:44.740
For example, maintenance is OK.

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:48.320
Support's not, or it's OK for a
given set of your environment.

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:52.120
So layer 6 is disk destroyal
or, as I like to call it,

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:54.400
hard drive graveyard.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:57.310
So Google employs a
strict equipment chain

00:17:57.310 --> 00:18:00.580
of custody monitoring,
meaning that we meticulously

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:03.280
track the location and the
status of all equipment

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:04.750
within our data center.

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:07.270
This means that we track
the location and status

00:18:07.270 --> 00:18:10.930
of every hard drive, from
acquisition to destruction,

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:13.810
using bar codes and asset tags.

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:16.180
These asset tags are scanned
throughout a hard drive's

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:18.370
lifecycle in a data
center from the time

00:18:18.370 --> 00:18:20.560
that it's installed
to the time that it's

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:22.690
removed from circulation.

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:25.450
And tracking these
hard drives closely

00:18:25.450 --> 00:18:27.790
ensures that they don't
go missing or end up

00:18:27.790 --> 00:18:30.410
in the wrong hands.

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:32.510
We also make sure hard
drives are properly

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:34.970
functioning by doing
frequent performance tests.

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:37.730
If a component fails to
pass a performance test,

00:18:37.730 --> 00:18:40.070
then it's deemed
no longer usable.

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:42.050
So to prevent any
sensitive information

00:18:42.050 --> 00:18:45.170
from living on that disk, then
we remove it from inventory

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:48.830
to be erased and destroyed
in layer 6, disk erase.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:52.610
Disks enter layer 6 through a
secure two-way locker system,

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:54.440
allowing only
authorized personnel

00:18:54.440 --> 00:19:00.040
to retrieve it in layer 6,
which you're seeing here.

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:02.170
When a hard drive is
erased and retired,

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:05.560
the disk erase formatter
uses a zero-filling method

00:19:05.560 --> 00:19:08.770
that wipes the disk data and
replaces each bit of data

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:09.730
with zeros.

00:19:09.730 --> 00:19:12.770
And we try to recycle
drives and their components.

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:15.460
But if a drive can't
be erased, then it's

00:19:15.460 --> 00:19:18.460
stored securely until it
can be physically destroyed,

00:19:18.460 --> 00:19:19.900
as you can see here.

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:22.270
And it'd be pretty
impossible to recover

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:24.520
any data from these drives.

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:27.250
And even if anyone tried to take
anything out of the data center

00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:29.860
floor with them, we also
have metal detectors

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:32.920
and video surveillance to
ensure all personnel exiting

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:34.660
the data center
halls are properly

00:19:34.660 --> 00:19:37.780
checked to prevent unauthorized
equipment from leaving

00:19:37.780 --> 00:19:39.200
the floor.

00:19:39.200 --> 00:19:41.360
If you're wondering what
happens to this waste,

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:43.910
we actually have researchers
in Google's Oklahoma facility

00:19:43.910 --> 00:19:46.970
who are looking into methods
of collecting and recycling

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:49.790
rare earth metals
in hard drives.

00:19:49.790 --> 00:19:52.550
Data centers are the largest
consumer of hard drives

00:19:52.550 --> 00:19:56.948
that contain elements like
neodymium and dysprosium.

00:19:56.948 --> 00:19:58.490
These are the same
elements that have

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:00.860
a role in the components
of electric cars.

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:02.930
And there's a great
article to check out

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:04.550
if you want to learn more.

00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:07.640
Leaving layers 5 and 6 is
arguably more difficult

00:20:07.640 --> 00:20:09.590
than entering, as
each person must

00:20:09.590 --> 00:20:11.450
exit through full
metal detection

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:13.250
and detailed examination.

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:16.460
And fun fact-- Google Cloud
also employs a security testing

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:19.970
program where undercover
actors are, in our employ,

00:20:19.970 --> 00:20:23.540
attempting to bring hardware
out of the data center.

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:26.810
This helps us constantly test
and improve our physical data

00:20:26.810 --> 00:20:28.700
center security techniques.

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:31.250
We actually run dozens
of these drills a year,

00:20:31.250 --> 00:20:33.740
paying unannounced,
skilled adversaries

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:36.080
to try to get past
controls by any means

00:20:36.080 --> 00:20:38.210
necessary except for violence.

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:42.470
And this includes fake badges,
social engineering, fake UPS

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:45.680
trucks, ladder trucks to
get over the fence, people

00:20:45.680 --> 00:20:49.520
hidden in trucks, attempts
in thick fog, everything.

00:20:49.520 --> 00:20:51.380
And we usually win.

00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:55.430
After every attempt, we actually
run a blameless post-mortem

00:20:55.430 --> 00:20:58.280
to improve and make the
next attempt even harder.

00:20:58.280 --> 00:21:00.590
And we ensure the strength
of our security controls

00:21:00.590 --> 00:21:02.760
and iterate controls as needed.

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:06.320
This is all about building
a culture of security.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:08.540
So these layers don't stop
at the physical layer.

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:11.030
They extend into our
technology stack that supports

00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:14.410
Google Cloud products, too.

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:16.960
So at Google Cloud, we provide
capabilities and assistance

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:20.590
across Google, Cloud,
Workspace, and connected devices

00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:22.870
that cover the whole
scope of responsibility.

00:21:22.870 --> 00:21:25.300
We have a number of
native security features

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:27.580
that help protect you
from vulnerabilities,

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.810
like encryption in
transit and at rest.

00:21:29.810 --> 00:21:33.160
And our hardware infrastructure
is custom-designed.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:35.980
Our servers don't include
any unnecessary components,

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:38.590
like video cards or
peripheral interconnects,

00:21:38.590 --> 00:21:41.080
that can introduce
any vulnerabilities.

00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:44.110
And the same goes for software,
including low-level software

00:21:44.110 --> 00:21:46.930
and our OS, which is a
stripped down, hardened

00:21:46.930 --> 00:21:49.000
version of Linux.

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:52.000
Further, we also design and
include hardware specifically

00:21:52.000 --> 00:21:54.970
for security, like Titan,
our custom security

00:21:54.970 --> 00:21:58.090
chip that we use to establish
a hardware root of trust

00:21:58.090 --> 00:21:59.920
in our servers and peripherals.

00:21:59.920 --> 00:22:02.080
We build our network
hardware and software

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:05.860
to improve performance
as well as security.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:10.150
So on Google Cloud, what's the
best way to approach security?

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:13.090
Well, cloud security is
a shared responsibility.

00:22:13.090 --> 00:22:15.160
Depending on what cloud
service you're using,

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:18.310
Google Cloud will manage
various parts of security

00:22:18.310 --> 00:22:21.490
above what we ensure through
our secure infrastructure.

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:25.540
So customers are responsible
for securing their own workloads

00:22:25.540 --> 00:22:28.300
to meet their requirements
and compliance.

00:22:28.300 --> 00:22:30.860
When it comes to cloud
security best practices,

00:22:30.860 --> 00:22:33.820
there are two sides to it,
preventative and architectural

00:22:33.820 --> 00:22:37.330
controls, which are implemented
through architecture and policy

00:22:37.330 --> 00:22:38.260
decisions.

00:22:38.260 --> 00:22:40.810
And first, we're going to cover
architectural controls, which

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:43.750
include the communication
between services,

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:46.240
how you structure your
resources on Google Cloud,

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:47.410
and permissions.

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:49.900
And then I'll quickly cover
detective controls, which

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:53.440
use monitoring capabilities
to look for drift, anomalous,

00:22:53.440 --> 00:22:57.950
or malicious behavior
as it happens.

00:22:57.950 --> 00:23:00.260
Let's talk about our
approach to security tooling

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:01.580
on Google Cloud.

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:03.920
We have layers of security
products and blueprints

00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:05.700
for you to quickly
get started here.

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:08.690
So from the bottom up,
we have a global scale

00:23:08.690 --> 00:23:11.990
technical infrastructure that's
designed to provide security

00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:13.880
throughout the entire
information processing

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:15.440
lifecycle at Google.

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:18.710
And this infrastructure provides
secure deployment of services,

00:23:18.710 --> 00:23:23.180
secure storage of data, secure
storage with end user privacy

00:23:23.180 --> 00:23:25.580
safeguards, secure
communications

00:23:25.580 --> 00:23:29.090
between services, and also
secure private communications

00:23:29.090 --> 00:23:32.480
with customers over the internet
as well as safe operation

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:35.070
by administrators.

00:23:35.070 --> 00:23:38.260
Building on top of the
secured core infrastructure,

00:23:38.260 --> 00:23:40.590
the next layer is the
security products,

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:43.170
like Security Command
Center Premium, which

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:45.360
is our security and risk
management platform,

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:48.090
and assured workloads for
securing your government

00:23:48.090 --> 00:23:51.270
workloads, which enable you
to secure your deployments.

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:54.300
These form the lowest levels
of the security stack.

00:23:54.300 --> 00:23:57.630
And then on top of this baseline
are security foundations

00:23:57.630 --> 00:23:58.620
blueprints.

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:00.330
These are security
blueprints which

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:03.180
include Google Cloud
security best practices that

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:04.840
are embedded within them.

00:24:04.840 --> 00:24:07.260
So our goal here is
to make it much easier

00:24:07.260 --> 00:24:11.430
for you to start and operate
securely on Google Cloud.

00:24:11.430 --> 00:24:14.130
They actually enable you to
jump-start your deployments

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:16.530
by handling some of
your key advanced needs

00:24:16.530 --> 00:24:20.100
without your teams first needing
to take time out and learn

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:22.460
all of the advanced skills.

00:24:22.460 --> 00:24:25.190
They help you set up org
policies as a resource

00:24:25.190 --> 00:24:30.650
hierarchy, networking, access,
logging, detective controls,

00:24:30.650 --> 00:24:32.450
and then posture
blueprints, which

00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:35.990
cover specific sets of
services and workloads.

00:24:35.990 --> 00:24:38.000
Blueprints also
come in three tiers,

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:41.270
which differ in their level of
native support and lifecycle

00:24:41.270 --> 00:24:42.740
management.

00:24:42.740 --> 00:24:46.220
Supported blueprints are created
by the Google security teams

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:48.680
and managed as
products, which are

00:24:48.680 --> 00:24:50.930
regular refreshes
and attestation

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:54.320
and testing of the
functionality and resilience.

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:55.640
They're all verified.

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:58.430
So blueprints can be created
by Google teams or partner

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:00.380
teams or even customer teams.

00:25:00.380 --> 00:25:02.270
And the key is that the
lifecycle management

00:25:02.270 --> 00:25:04.760
and attestation
of fit for purpose

00:25:04.760 --> 00:25:07.190
is the responsibility
of the creating team.

00:25:07.190 --> 00:25:10.340
And today, they're
offered via Terraform.

00:25:10.340 --> 00:25:12.960
So compatible blueprints
can be created by anyone.

00:25:12.960 --> 00:25:14.360
The requirement
is just that they

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:16.280
need to be correctly
formatted to operate

00:25:16.280 --> 00:25:18.458
through the blueprint
tool chains.

00:25:18.458 --> 00:25:20.000
And then we also
have blueprints that

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:23.120
address specific use cases,
like protecting storage buckets

00:25:23.120 --> 00:25:24.410
or doing--

00:25:24.410 --> 00:25:28.670
protecting BigQuery users, AI
notebooks, or Anthos, even.

00:25:28.670 --> 00:25:31.760
So the topmost layer
here is comprised also

00:25:31.760 --> 00:25:35.600
of your own web app and API
protection services, analytics,

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:38.990
or even high-performance
computing environments.

00:25:38.990 --> 00:25:41.090
So the security
foundations blueprints

00:25:41.090 --> 00:25:43.640
provides a strong starting
point for customers

00:25:43.640 --> 00:25:46.500
to build and deploy
their organization.

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:49.430
So customers have access
to the opinionated guide

00:25:49.430 --> 00:25:52.310
of the full Terraform
repo of automation scripts

00:25:52.310 --> 00:25:54.470
to deploy the
foundational landing zone.

00:25:54.470 --> 00:25:57.260
And it can be deployed
either at the full org level

00:25:57.260 --> 00:25:59.870
or at the folder level
in the hierarchy.

00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:02.750
These blueprints provide an
opinionated vetting pattern

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:05.840
for resource hierarchy,
org policies, IAM,

00:26:05.840 --> 00:26:09.620
separation of duties, network
architecture, data access

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:13.340
segmentation, logging,
and detective controls

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:15.110
across projects and accounts.

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:18.380
So that way, you can meet
your security compliance

00:26:18.380 --> 00:26:20.300
and have a starting point.

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:23.240
And then you can also start
to build your own workloads

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:25.640
and projects on top.

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:28.190
It provides the foundation
upon which you can further

00:26:28.190 --> 00:26:31.700
deploy an increasing number
of opinionated Google-created

00:26:31.700 --> 00:26:34.550
security posture blueprints
for specific products,

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:36.440
sets of services, and workloads.

00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:39.380
And we update these blueprints
on a six-month cycle.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:42.440
We also provide a project
factory Terraform module

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:44.390
that enables you to
set the parameters

00:26:44.390 --> 00:26:47.790
and constraints for projects
when they are created.

00:26:47.790 --> 00:26:50.360
You can access the latest
reference guide, PDF,

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:53.480
and the Terraform scripts repo
from the Google Cloud Security

00:26:53.480 --> 00:26:55.340
Best Practices Resource Center.

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:58.670
And here, in addition to the
curated set of Google Cloud

00:26:58.670 --> 00:27:00.620
security blueprints,
you can also

00:27:00.620 --> 00:27:02.870
access both the
Google BeyondProd

00:27:02.870 --> 00:27:05.450
paper, which lays out the
principles and practices

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:08.330
of Google's approach for
Cloud-native security

00:27:08.330 --> 00:27:12.627
and how we think about and
how we operate Cloud-native.

00:27:12.627 --> 00:27:14.960
For customers who want to
dive deeper into the knowledge

00:27:14.960 --> 00:27:18.020
and experience that Google
has, then the Resource Center

00:27:18.020 --> 00:27:21.110
also includes a link to
our building security

00:27:21.110 --> 00:27:24.080
and reliable systems
reference book.

00:27:24.080 --> 00:27:26.140
Additionally, a range
of detailed background

00:27:26.140 --> 00:27:28.780
white papers around
our reference materials

00:27:28.780 --> 00:27:31.750
is available to customers
from that Resource Center.

00:27:31.750 --> 00:27:34.840
And it covers topics like
infrastructure design

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:38.380
and how encryption at
rest and in transit

00:27:38.380 --> 00:27:40.750
are handled on Google Cloud.

00:27:40.750 --> 00:27:42.340
On the preventative
side, we also

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:44.020
launched Security
Command Center.

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:46.660
This is the canonical
security and risk

00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:48.550
database for Google Cloud.

00:27:48.550 --> 00:27:53.590
So it includes a risk
dashboard and analytics system

00:27:53.590 --> 00:27:56.050
for surfacing, understanding,
and remediating

00:27:56.050 --> 00:28:00.730
Google Cloud security and data
risk across your organization.

00:28:00.730 --> 00:28:02.220
So this means from
one dashboard,

00:28:02.220 --> 00:28:04.620
you can uncover
common web application

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:07.470
and vulnerabilities, like
cross-site scripting or

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:09.870
outdated libraries in
your web applications

00:28:09.870 --> 00:28:13.920
running on App Engine,
GKE, and Compute Engine.

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:15.570
And you can also
detect threats using

00:28:15.570 --> 00:28:18.900
logs running in Google Cloud
or common container attacks,

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:22.290
including suspicious binary
or suspicious library

00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:25.070
and reverse shell.

00:28:25.070 --> 00:28:28.070
Security Health Analytics is a
vulnerabilities dashboard here.

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:30.260
And it helps you view
security misconfigurations

00:28:30.260 --> 00:28:33.860
by severity, compliance
standards, or project.

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:37.870
And you can take
action from there.

00:28:37.870 --> 00:28:41.160
So with this, you can take
inventory of your Google Cloud

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:43.080
assets and partner solutions.

00:28:43.080 --> 00:28:46.200
You can also understand exactly
what changed in your asset

00:28:46.200 --> 00:28:49.560
history in near real time and
respond to the most pressing

00:28:49.560 --> 00:28:50.910
issues first.

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:52.710
You can also receive
notifications

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:55.920
about new findings or updates
to findings within minutes

00:28:55.920 --> 00:28:58.590
and be able to take action.

00:28:58.590 --> 00:29:01.770
And also, you can identify
security misconfigurations

00:29:01.770 --> 00:29:04.230
in your Google Cloud
assets and resolve them

00:29:04.230 --> 00:29:06.660
by following actionable
recommendations

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:08.650
that they actually have there.

00:29:08.650 --> 00:29:10.710
Lastly, you can catch
web vulnerabilities

00:29:10.710 --> 00:29:14.610
before they hit production and
reduce your exposure to risks

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:18.720
and identify compliance-- so see
violations in your Google Cloud

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:21.720
assets and resolve them
with these recommendations.

00:29:21.720 --> 00:29:23.890
And of course, you want
to be able to export them.

00:29:23.890 --> 00:29:25.500
So you can export
compliance reports

00:29:25.500 --> 00:29:27.833
to ensure all your resources
are meeting your compliance

00:29:27.833 --> 00:29:28.830
requirements.

00:29:28.830 --> 00:29:31.260
We have support for
compliance standards,

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:33.510
like Payment Card
Industry, which

00:29:33.510 --> 00:29:36.330
is PCI, the International
Organization

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:38.550
for Standardization, ISO.

00:29:38.550 --> 00:29:41.520
There's National Institute of
Standards and Technology, NIST,

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:44.340
and the Center for
Internet Security, CIS,

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:47.850
1 and 1.1, and benchmarks.

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:49.920
This industry-leading
threat intelligence

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:51.930
detects threats
using logs running

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:53.610
in Google Cloud at scale.

00:29:53.610 --> 00:29:56.370
And it also uses
kernel-level instrumentation

00:29:56.370 --> 00:29:59.430
to identify potential
compromises of containers

00:29:59.430 --> 00:30:01.710
and, lastly, combines
threat intelligence

00:30:01.710 --> 00:30:03.810
from first- and
third-party providers

00:30:03.810 --> 00:30:06.090
to better protect
your enterprise

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:09.720
from costly and
high-risk threats.

00:30:09.720 --> 00:30:11.548
Thank you so much for
joining this session.

00:30:11.548 --> 00:30:13.090
I know there's a
lot packed in there.

00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:15.720
So I hope you found it
as much of an adventure

00:30:15.720 --> 00:30:17.370
as it felt like
to go through it.

00:30:17.370 --> 00:30:19.260
If you do want to
learn more, check out

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:21.630
the resources that we'll
include for this session.

00:30:21.630 --> 00:30:24.900
And always feel free to
reach out to me on social,

00:30:24.900 --> 00:30:28.200
like on Twitter, @stephr_wong.

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:30.140
Thanks again.

